  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. It requires identifying the outcome with the minimum absolute value across both lotteries, treating it as a 'sure outcome', and then computing the differences between this sure outcome and the other outcomes, weighted by their probabilities. In this case, both lotteries include 0 Euros as an outcome. Since 0 is the smallest (and only common non-positive) outcome, it is treated as the 'sure outcome'. The differences are then: for Lottery A, 4000 - 0 = 4000 (with 20% probability); for Lottery B, 3000 - 0 = 3000 (with 25% probability). The weighted differences are: A: 0.20 × 4000 = 800; B: 0.25 × 3000 = 750. The sum of the sure outcome (0) and the weighted difference is 800 for A and 750 for B. Thus, the reason would prefer Lottery A, as it has the more favorable sum (800 > 750).",
